Yes. Based on the **actual ACLs and security policies we implemented**, the authorization view can be summarized like this:

### 🔐 User Authorization by VLAN

| VLAN / User                  | Can Do                                                                            | Cannot Do                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 🎮 **Gaming — VLAN 10**      | Access gaming resources, other permitted networks, Internet/external services     | ❌ Cannot initiate traffic to **Management VLAN 99**                                                        |
| 👨‍💼 **Staff — VLAN 20**    | Access normal network resources, services, Internet/external services             | ❌ Cannot initiate traffic to **Management VLAN 99**                                                        |
| 📶 **Guest Wi-Fi — VLAN 30** | Get DHCP address, access Internet/public network, use permitted external services | ❌ Cannot access **any internal 192.168.0.0/16 network**, including Gaming, Staff, Services, and Management |
| 🖥️ **Services — VLAN 40**   | Host/provide network services to permitted networks                               | ❌ No user access policy was specifically restricted by ACL yet                                             |
| 🔧 **Management — VLAN 99**  | Manage switches and access other network segments                                 | ✅ Trusted VLAN; no restriction preventing it from reaching other VLANs                                     |

### In simple terms

```text
                 GAMING     STAFF      GUEST      SERVICES
                   ↓          ↓           ↓           ↓
Management 99      ❌         ❌          ❌          —
                   ↑
             MANAGEMENT
              VLAN 99
          Trusted / Admin
```

And for Guest:

```text
Guest VLAN 30
      │
      ├──→ Internet / Public Network    ✅
      │
      └──→ Internal 192.168.0.0/16      ❌
              ├── Gaming
              ├── Staff
              ├── Services
              └── Management
```

**Important:** this is a **network authorization policy based on VLAN membership**, not user authentication. The VLAN determines the network segment the device belongs to, while the ACLs enforce what traffic that segment is allowed to initiate.

